<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Minoan civilisation</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 3000–1100 BCE</span> · Also known as Minoans, Minoan civilization</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Minoan civilisation was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> civilisation on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, named by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-evans">Arthur Evans</a> after the legendary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minos">King Minos</a>. From about 2000 BCE it built great palace complexes at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phaistos">Phaistos</a>, Malia and Zakros, used the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-a">Linear A</a> script and traded widely across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> and the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. After about 1450 BCE Crete came under Mycenaean Greek rule, and the palaces were destroyed or abandoned.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/minoan-civilisation.jpg" alt="Minoan civilisation" width="320" height="378" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wolfgang Sauber · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AMI_-_Oktopusvase.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
